🤖 Model Card: Black-Box Optimisation (BBO) Strategy

🧠 Model Overview
Model Name: Black-Box Optimisation Strategy
Type: Optimisation / Decision Strategy
Framework: Iterative, gradient-free optimisation
Author: Your Name
Date: 2026
This project implements a black-box optimisation strategy designed to identify high-performing input parameters for unknown objective functions. The model does not have access to internal system logic and relies entirely on observed input-output relationships.
🎯 Intended Use
Primary Use
Optimising unknown or complex systems where internal structure is not accessible
Hyperparameter tuning in machine learning
Industrial optimisation problems
Out of Scope
Situations requiring interpretability of internal model logic
Fully deterministic or known analytical systems
⚙️ Model Description
The optimisation strategy is based on:
Iterative query generation
Performance-based updates
Local search heuristics
Controlled exploration
Key Components
Input parameter generator
Evaluation loop (query → score)
Update mechanism (directional refinement)
🔄 Training / Optimisation Process
Although not trained in a traditional sense, the model evolves through:
Initial random exploration
Pattern identification from results
Local optimisation using directional updates
Refinement with small perturbations
This process is conceptually similar to:
Reinforcement learning (trial-and-error learning)
Bayesian optimisation (guided search)
📊 Data
Source
Generated during BBO competition iterations
Characteristics
Small dataset (limited queries)
Continuous numerical inputs
Output scores may contain noise
📈 Performance
Evaluation Metric
Objective score returned by the black-box function
Observed Behaviour
Gradual improvement across iterations
Reduced variance in later stages
Convergence toward high-performing regions
⚖️ Trade-offs
Aspect	Description
Exploration	Needed early to understand search space
Exploitation	Needed later for refinement
Stability vs Speed	Smaller steps improve stability but slow progress
⚠️ Limitations
No access to internal function behaviour
Sensitive to noise in outputs
Limited sample size
May converge to local optima
🔍 Ethical Considerations
No personal or sensitive data used
No bias concerns in traditional sense (non-human data)
However, optimisation strategies could introduce bias if applied to real-world decision systems
